When a sleeping node sends a Wake Up Notification, the system marks it as awake and executes compatibility queries if configured. However, when these compat queries timeout due to the node going back to sleep, the node was incorrectly left marked as awake.

## Problem

The issue occurs in the `compatDoWakeupQueries` function in `WakeUpCC.ts`. When a compat query times out with `ZWaveErrorCodes.Controller_MessageExpired`, the function returns early without updating the node's sleep status:

```typescript
if (
    isZWaveError(e)
    && e.code === ZWaveErrorCodes.Controller_MessageExpired
) {
    // A compat query expired - no point in trying the others too
    return; // ❌ Node remains marked as awake
}
```

This leaves the node incorrectly marked as awake even though the timeout indicates it has likely gone back to sleep. Subsequent commands then fail unnecessarily because the system believes the node is awake when it's actually asleep.

## Solution

Added a call to `node.markAsAsleep()` when a compat query times out:

```typescript
if (
    isZWaveError(e)
    && e.code === ZWaveErrorCodes.Controller_MessageExpired
) {
    // A compat query expired - no point in trying the others too
    // Mark the node as asleep since it likely went back to sleep
    node.markAsAsleep();
    return;
}
```

This ensures that when a compat query times out, the node's status correctly reflects that it has returned to sleep, preventing unnecessary command failures and improving overall system behavior.

## Testing

- All existing node asleep-related tests continue to pass
- All WakeUp CC tests continue to pass  
- All node status machine tests continue to pass
- No regressions detected in related functionality

Fixes #6501.

