This PR changes the behavior of the linter from disallowing global variables entirely to allowing them but disallowing mutations of global variables after initialization.

## Motivation
Global variables can cause side effects that are difficult to track when they are mutated. However, many Go code patterns use globals as pseudo-constants, which don't cause side effects when they're not mutated after initialization.

## Implementation Details
- Modified the analyzer to track all global variables in the package
- Added detection of assignments, increments, and decrements of globals in function scope
- Carefully handles variable shadowing to avoid false positives
- Created new test cases specifically for testing global variable mutations
- Updated documentation to reflect the new behavior

## Example of new behavior

The linter now accepts:
```go
// Global variables are allowed as long as they're not mutated
var Config = loadConfig()
var DefaultClient = &http.Client{Timeout: 30 * time.Second}

func main() {
    // Use the globals, but don't mutate them
    resp, err := DefaultClient.Get("https://example.com")
    // ...
}
```

But flags this code:
```go
var counter = 0

func incrementCounter() {
    counter++ // Error: global variable counter is being mutated
}

func resetCounter() {
    counter = 0 // Error: global variable counter is being mutated
}
```

This change allows globals to be used as pseudo-constants while still preventing the side effects that arise from mutations.

Fixes #41.

